  151: **THE DEFECT.** `horrible_getputpixel()`'s RGB arms are not inverses of each other:
  152: 
  153:     PUT RGB   buf[0] = c>>24;  buf[1] = c>>16;  buf[2] = c>>8;  buf[3] = 0;
  154:     GET RGB   *color = (buf[1] << 24) + (buf[2] << 16) + (buf[3] << 8);
  155: 
  156: PUT lays the three channels at bytes 0, 1, 2 and an explicit pad at 3. GET reads 1, 2, 3 - the pad
  157: included, the red channel discarded. **RGB is the only 32-bit colour mode in the file that fails its own
  158: round trip**, and a copy with an RGB source turns `0xaabbccdd` into `0xbbccdd00`.
  159: 
  160: **WITNESS, rung 3, on an unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32`:** `regress/artifacts/sgirergbroundtrip/rgb_witness.py`.
  161: Both ladder controls are present and were green in every run - `live` (a guest store and load through RAM)
  162: and `devsig` (`CRIME_DE_STATUS` reads `0x1e000000` where RAM reads 0) - plus a sibling accept control,
  163: `copy lin_a`, which drives an **RGBA** source through the same helper and proves a repair did not break the
  164: neighbour.
  165: 
  166: ---
  167: 
  168: ## *** THE PANEL REFUTED MY ATTRIBUTION, AND IT WAS RIGHT TO. ***
  169: 
  170: The brief argued the GET is the wrong arm on two grounds. Four seats attacked both, independently:
  171: 
  172: * **Ground 1 was CIRCULAR.** *"PUT's `buf[3] = 0` is an explicit pad write, so a GET reading byte 3 reads
  173:   the pad"* - that assumes PUT is authoritative, which is exactly the question.
  174: * **Ground 2 was NEUTRALISED.** I appealed to RGBA's byte order; ABGR uses the opposite one, **and the
  175:   default/raw GET arm uses ABGR order too**, so the file offers no single order to appeal to.
  176: * **The measurement is ARM-NEUTRAL.** `0xbbccdd00` is simply what the code does. Under a pad-FIRST
  177:   container it is the *faithful* result and PUT is the bug. **A round-trip failure cannot say which end
  178:   failed** - which is the whole reason this needed a panel and not a fix.
  179: 
  180: ### The settling fact is in `dev_sgi_gbe.c`, and no seat found it
  181: 
  182: The scanout reads the same tile memory back for display:
  183: 
  184:     color = (buf[i]<<24) + (buf[i+1]<<16) + (buf[i+2]<<8) + buf[i+3];   /* byte 0 = MSB */
  185:     case CRMFB_MODE_TYP_RGB8:      // Used by NetBSD's X11 server
  186:             *r = color >> 24;  *g = color >> 16;  *b = color >> 8;      /* byte 3 UNUSED */
  187: 
  188: **R = byte 0, G = byte 1, B = byte 2, and byte 3 is ignored - exactly what the PUT arm writes.** So the pad
  189: sits at `buf[3]` *because the consumer ignores `buf[3]`*, not because PUT writes a zero there. Different
  190: file, opposite direction, and independent of which DE arm is right: that is what makes it non-circular.
  191: 
  192: ### And glm dissolved the ABGR objection from the other end
  193: 
  194: > *"for the same color, RGBA and ABGR write **identical memory bytes**, `[R,G,B,A]`; they differ only in
  195: > register word order . among the five 4-byte arms, the RGB GET is the lone container outlier - your case
  196: > is stronger than you stated."*
  197: 
  198: Verified by reading: RGBA word `0xRRGGBBAA`  `buf[RR,GG,BB,AA]`; ABGR word `0xAABBGGRR` 
  199: `buf[RR,GG,BB,AA]`. **My "opposite byte orders" claim was about the register WORD. The question is about
  200: the memory CONTAINER, and on the container all three 4-byte PUT arms agree.** The objection I had invited
  201: turned into the strongest supporting argument, from the seat that raised it.
  202: 
  203: **codex's reservation is kept and is in the contract:** *"GET is the best-supported repair site for this
  204: emulator's RGB layout; hardware fidelity and real-driver usage remain unproved."* No CRIME/RE datasheet is
  205: in `_scratchpad/` - checked by `ls`, not assumed - so this ships as an **internal-consistency fix warranted
  206: by the tree's own consumer**, never as a claim about silicon.
  207: 
  208: ---
  209: 
  210: ## *** THE PANEL ALSO CAUGHT MY ORACLE, AND IT WOULD HAVE FAILED A CORRECT FIX. ***
  211: 
  212: The witness's success row pinned **`0xaabbccdd`**, the source word. A corrected RGB GET returns
  213: **`0xaabbcc00`** - RGB carries three channels and the pad is not transported, which PUT's own `buf[3] = 0`
  214: says from the other side.
  215: 
  216: **Four seats named `0xaabbcc00` independently** - grok, glm, the kimi substitute, and codex - against a
  217: brief that had stated otherwise. The row was re-pinned **before** the code changed and the committed build
  218: re-measured RED under the new expectation, which is the oracle-correction rule's order discriminator
  219: satisfied rather than asserted.
  220: 
  221: *The surviving row is stronger for it: `rgb chan` pins the exact post-fix value, so a fix that moves the
  222: bytes to some **third** wrong place still reds - which a "not the corrupt value" row could never do.*
  223: 
  224: **This is the second time in one day that I built a conclusion on a premise I had not thought to
  225: question** - the first was `sgilinearnegidx`, withdrawn at `a264a34` because a variable's declared type
  226: three lines above the code I quoted refuted it. There the premise was a type; here it was which arm is
  227: authoritative. The queued rule question `sgilinearnegidxpostmortem` asks whether the premise-audit
  228: instruction needs a self-directed twin, and this round is its second instance.
  229: 
  230: ---
  231: 
  232: ## Residuals filed, none of them the round's subject
  233: 
  234: All three came from seats volunteering something under the found-something-else licence:
  235: 
  236: * **`sgirgbscanoutrow`** - the round's strongest evidence is a claim about `dev_sgi_gbe.c` that **no row
  237:   observes**. A row would need a framebuffer oracle, i.e. a different probe shape. Filed as an explicit
  238:   no-row sentence by the PRE-TABLE CLAIMROW check rather than omitted.
  239: * **`sgirebufshiftub`** - `buf[i] << 24` on a `uint8_t` promotes to **signed** `int`, so any byte � 128
  240:   is a signed-shift overflow (UB). Pre-existing on all four GET arms; `#505` replaced one instance of the
  241:   idiom with another, which is the right minimal-surgical choice. Filed, and grok agrees it is *"not a
  242:   reason to block"*.
  243: * **`sgirebufuninit` SHARPENED** - glm widened it from one exposure to three, and the third was new: at
  244:   `bufdepth` 1 or 2 the RGB/RGBA/ABGR GETs read bytes that the guest's own read never filled. `#505`'s own
  245:   GUARD-FIELD-vs-BODY-FIELD check found the same disagreeing state and **declined to row it** - a row here
  246:   would attribute a pre-existing defect to a round that neither created nor cured it.
  247: 
  248:   > #### *** THIS BULLET FIRST READ *"the RGB/RGBA/ABGR GETs read `buf[2..3]`"* AND GAVE THE DECLINE'S
  249:   > #### REASON AS *"the exposure is identical before and after this edit"*.  **BOTH ARE FALSE OF RGB, AND
  250:   > #### `#505` IS WHAT FALSIFIED THEM.**  Corrected 2026-09-07, before commit. ***
  251:   >
  252:   > Three seats on the `sgirebufuninit` panel said the read-side sentence was too broad - grok: *"its GET
  253:   > sentence is false of RGB as the file sits"*; glm: *"asserted in prose, not in the enumerated table, and
  254:   > under the packet's own model it is wrong"*; minimax: *"the table is asymmetric - write direction
  255:   > enumerated, read direction mentioned in one sentence only."*  The codex seat then supplied the
  256:   > per-arm table, and it is **verified here by reading `dev_sgi_re.c`'s GET arms**:
  257:   >
  258:   > | GET arm | unassigned bytes consumed after a 1-byte read | after 2 bytes | after 4 or 8 |
  259:   > |---|---|---|---|
  260:   > | CI | none | none | none |
  261:   > | **RGB** | `buf[1..2]` | **`buf[2]`** | none |
  262:   > | RGBA / ABGR / default | `buf[1..3]` | `buf[2..3]` | none |
  263:   >
  264:   > **RGB never touches `buf[3]` any more, because this round is what stopped it**: the GET went from
  265:   > `buf[1..3]` to `buf[0..2]`.  So the exposure is **not** identical before and after - for RGB this
  266:   > round *reduced* it by one byte.
  267:   >
  268:   > **The DECLINE still stands and is now better warranted**: the round did not create or worsen the
  269:   > uninitialised read, it shrank it, and the remaining exposure is pre-existing on arms this diff does
  270:   > not touch.  **What was wrong was the REASON, and a wrong reason gets reused** - this project has
  271:   > twice cited one before anyone reopened the file.
  272:   >
  273:   > *Worth naming plainly: the false sentence was written INSIDE the round that falsified it.  A block
  274:   > describing a fix inherits the pre-fix file's facts unless each one is re-read against the post-fix
  275:   > source, and nothing prompts that re-reading - the diff looks like the thing that changed, and the
  276:   > prose looks like the thing that describes it.*
  277: 
