  366: 
  367: ### *** THE FLOOR BREACH, AND IT WAS IN MY OWN DETECTOR. L2, REPAIRED IN-ROUND. ***
  368: 
  369: `M6_cond` is the canonical clause-(d) form - guard kept, repair conditioned on a field the guarded
  370: body reads:
  371: 
  372: ```c
  373: 	case DE_MODE_TYPE_RGB:
  374: 		if (bufdepth == 4)  *color = (buf[0]<<24)+(buf[1]<<16)+(buf[2]<<8);   /* the fix */
  375: 		else                *color = (buf[1]<<24)+(buf[2]<<16)+(buf[3]<<8);   /* the defect */
  376: ```
  377: 
  378: **Every session in the 6-row detector used `BUFDEPTH_32`, so `bufdepth` was 4 in all of them and the
  379: mutant's fix branch was the only one the detector ever executed.** It proved the fix works exactly
  380: where I measured it and said nothing whatever about anywhere else. `bufdepth` is not an exotic
  381: choice: it is read by the guarded body (it is `memory_rw`'s transfer length) and comes from **bits
  382: 8-9 of the same mode word** whose bits 4-7 the guard reads. The DEPENDS-ON closure enumerates it
  383: first, not last.
  384: 
  385: **Repair: `rgb depth64`, one extra session at `BUFDEPTH_64`.** Width 8 keeps `buf[0..2]` filled so
  386: the row stays a *value* oracle; widths 1 and 2 would read uninitialised bytes (`sgirebufuninit`) and
  387: make it non-deterministic - **8 is the only other width that can carry a value oracle at all.**
  388: 
  389: ### *** AND THE MUTANT I BUILT TO CHECK FOR THE `#499` SHAPE WAS ITSELF MIS-BUILT.  Pass 2 caught it. ***
  390: 
  391: I added `M7_cond8` to test whether I had repaired only the cell M6 named, and recorded its 7/8 as
  392: proof the width axis was closed. **The kimi substitute - a packet-fed seat that cannot read the
  393: repo - refuted it from the packet's own arithmetic:**
  394: 
  395: > *"`M7_cond8` cannot be both 'the mirror of M6' and score 7/8; a mirror predicts 5/8."*
  396: 
  397: ```
  398: M6_cond   if (bufdepth == 4) FIXED else DEFECT   ->  width 4 fixed, width 8 defective
  399: M7_cond8  if (bufdepth != 8) FIXED else DEFECT   ->  width 4 fixed, width 8 defective
  400: ```
  401: 
  402: **Over the only two widths any row drives, {4, 8}, those are the same function.** M7 re-measured M6.
  403: 
  404: **MEASURED, table 3 (`killtable3_20260907/`): the true mirror `M9_truemirror`
  405: (`if (bufdepth == 8) FIXED else DEFECT`) scores 5/8** - exactly what kimi derived - with three
  406: width-4 rows red. **So the width axis is closed, but by `M9`, not by the mutant this block first
  407: credited.** The conclusion survives; its evidence did not, and a claim resting on a mutant that
  408: measures nothing is a wrong record however right the conclusion turns out to be.
  409: 
  410: **Pass 2 also named LOCATION as an unpinned axis** and predicted `if (ofs == K)` would sweep 8/8,
  411: since every row writes at ofs 0. **`M10_ofs` reds four rows instead** - because the source pixel is
  412: read at `src_x = 0x40`, so `ofs` is 0x40 on every GET and 0 only on the destination PUT. The rows
  413: discriminate on location already, **by an accident of the probe's geometry rather than by design**,
  414: which is recorded as exactly that: covered, unintended, and fragile to anyone who moves `SRC_OFS`.
  415: 
  416: ### `M4_or` is an equivalent mutant, which is a result and not a hole
  417: 
  418: `+`  `|` on `(buf[0]<<24) + (buf[1]<<16) + (buf[2]<<8)`. The three fields are disjoint - bits 24-31,
  419: 16-23, 8-15 - so `+`, `|` and `^` compute the same value. Verified two ways: enumerated over 80 byte
  420: triples with zero disagreements, and its row values are byte-identical to base. Of the three
  421: explanations the carrier gives for a mutant that will not die, this is *"the target is dead"*: there
  422: is no behavioural difference for any test to detect. *Honest caveat: that derivation assumes
  423: well-defined arithmetic, and `sgirebufshiftub` records that `buf[i]<<24` on a `uint8_t` is
  424: signed-shift UB for bytes � 128. The measurement settles it for this compiler; the derivation alone
  425: would not.*
