  452: ### *** KILL TABLE 4: A SECOND FLOOR BREACH, ON AN AXIS NOBODY HAD LOOKED AT. ***
  453: 
  454: `regress/artifacts/sgirergbroundtrip/killtable4_20260907/`:
  455: 
  456: | run | score | |
  457: |---|---|---|
  458: | base | 8/8 | |
  459: | **`M12_linear`** | **8/8** | *** CLEAN SWEEP - clause (d) floor breach *** |
  460: 
  461: ```c
  462: 	case DE_MODE_TYPE_RGB:
  463: 		if (linear)  *color = (buf[0]<<24)+(buf[1]<<16)+(buf[2]<<8);   /* the fix    */
  464: 		else         *color = (buf[1]<<24)+(buf[2]<<16)+(buf[3]<<8);   /* the defect */
  465: ```
  466: 
  467: **Every row in the detector drove LIN_A**, so `linear` was true in all eight and a repair conditioned
  468: on it applied everywhere the detector looked. `linear` is `tlb_mode > 3` and it **dominates** the
  469: edited lines through the `if (!linear && (x < 0 || .)) return;` guard, so it is inside the DEPENDS-ON
  470: closure - a floor breach, not coverage debt. **L2, repaired in-round** by `rgb tiled`, a ninth row
  471: driving a TLB_A source.
  472: 
  473: **Turning the prediction into a measurement required correcting the seat's form.** deepseek proposed
  474: conditioning on `tileptr`; that cannot discriminate addressing mode, because
  475: `tileptr &= ~0x80000000` strips the linear flag **before** the switch. `linear` is the variable the
  476: source keeps, and building the mutant in the shape the code permits is what made it measurable.
  477: 
  478: **AND THE NEW ROW CAUGHT MY OWN SETUP ERROR ON ITS FIRST RUN, which is what a row is for.** It read
  479: `0x77665544` - `FG_VAL`, which this probe's own `DE_FG` comment names as *"what a declined GET
  480: leaves"*. The cause: `re_tlb_a` is `uint16_t[256]` filled by a **halfword** loop
  481: (`d->re_tlb_a[tlbi] = data[hwi]*256 + data[hwi+1]`), not `uint32_t` like `re_linear_a`. The `<< 32`
  482: shift that is correct for LINEAR_A lands in `re_tlb_a[2]` and leaves `[0]` at zero, giving
  483: `tileptr = 0` and a declined access. Corrected to `<< 48`. **A row that passed quietly here would
  484: have been vacuous by construction - it would have measured a declined access, not a tiled one.**
  485: 
  486: ### *** BUILDING THE ROW THAT CLOSES THE BREACH TOOK TWO CORRECTIONS, AND THE ROW REPORTED BOTH. ***
  487: 
  488: `rgb tiled` went red twice before it went green, and both times it read exactly `0x77665544` -
  489: `FG_VAL`, which this probe's own `DE_FG` comment names as *"what a declined GET leaves"*. **The value
  490: was self-documenting: the GET was never reaching the RGB arm at all.**
  491: 
  492: **Correction 1 - the array is halfwords, not words.**
  493: 
  494: ```c
  495: 	uint16_t	re_tlb_a[256];              /* vs uint32_t re_linear_a[32] */
  496: 	...
  497: 	for (size_t hwi = 0; hwi < len; hwi += sizeof(uint16_t)) {
  498: 		d->re_tlb_a[tlbi] = data[hwi]*256 + data[hwi+1];
  499: 		tlbi++;
  500: 	}
  501: ```
  502: 
  503: An 8-byte store fills `re_tlb_a[0..3]` from four big-endian halfwords, so the element that lands in
  504: `[0]` is bits 63..48. The `<< 32` shift that is **correct for `RE_LINEAR_A`** - because
  505: `re_linear_a` is `uint32_t` - puts the value in `re_tlb_a[2]` and leaves `[0]` at zero.
  506: 
  507: **Correction 2 - a tiled tile pointer must carry its own valid bit, and the linear path hides that.**
  508: 
  509: ```c
  510: 	// The highest bit seems to be set for a "valid" tile pointer.
  511: 	if (!(tileptr & 0x80000000))
  512: 		return;                          /* silent decline */
  513: 	tileptr &= ~0x80000000;
  514: ```
  515: 
  516: `LIN_A` sets that bit **explicitly** - `tileptr = 0x80000000 | (re_linear_a[n] << 12)` - so a probe
  517: built from the linear path never learns it exists. `TLB_A` takes it from the stored value:
  518: `tileptr = re_tlb_a[n] << 16`, and `re_tlb_a` is `uint16_t`, so bit 31 of `tileptr` **is bit 15 of the
  519: register**. `re_tlb_a[0] = 2` yields `0x00020000`, the guard rejects it, and the GET returns without
  520: touching `*color`.
  521: 
  522: Fixed by `re_tlb_a[0] = 0x8002`, giving `tileptr = 0x80020000`  valid  masked to `0x20000`.
  523: 
  524: > **A ROW THAT HAD GONE GREEN THROUGH EITHER MISTAKE WOULD HAVE BEEN VACUOUS BY CONSTRUCTION** - it
  525: > would have measured a *declined* access while claiming to measure a *tiled* one, which is the
  526: > refusal-effect vacuity `#489` paid for four times. It went red instead, twice, and named the value
  527: > that explained why.
  528: 
  529: *And there is a lesson about the breach itself: `M12_linear` swept 8/8 precisely because no row had
  530: ever driven the non-linear path - and closing it required two facts about the device that the linear
  531: path conceals. **An axis nothing exercises is also an axis nobody has had to learn**, which is why
  532: the cost of closing one is systematically underestimated.*
  533: 
  534: ### *** PASS 3 REFUSED TO CLOSE THE CHAIN, AND IT WAS RIGHT: A THIRD FLOOR BREACH. ***
  535: 
  536: The repair-chain rule says a repair made in response to a pass 2 owes its own pass, and that the chain
  537: closes on the first pass finding **zero** breaches. Pass 3 found one, and **five seats declined to
  538: ratify** - codex (`NOT ESTABLISHED YET | OPEN`), grok (`NOT RATIFY`), minimax, deepseek and the kimi
  539: substitute (`NOT CLOSED`).
  540: 
  541: **THE BREACH IS A JOINT CELL, and it is the documented consequence of what I did.** Two axes had each
  542: been closed on their own:
  543: 
