 succeeded in 552ms:
    1: # `#508` alphapfnoffset: the Alpha page-table walk returned a page BASE, dropping the offset
    2: 
    3: **THE DEFECT.** `memory_alpha.c`, after the third-level PTE validates:
    4: 
    5:     (*return_paddr) = ALPHA_PTE_TO_PFN(pte3) << pageSizeShift;
    6: 
    7: `memory_rw.c` then takes `offset = paddr & offset_mask`, which is therefore **always zero** - so every
    8: walked access resolved to the page's first bytes. A codex reachability pass enumerated all 8192
    9: offsets: the walk yields offset zero throughout, differing from the offset-preserving address for
   10: **8191** of them.
   11: 
   12: **WITNESS, rung 3, unmodified `-E alpha -e alphabook1`:** `regress/alpha_pfnoffset_probe.py`.
   13: **HEAD 5/7** (`probe_head7_20260907.out`), **fix 7/7, same script** (`probe_fix_20260907.out`).
   14: A cold guest `ldq` through a hand-built three-level page table:
   15: 
   16: | row | HEAD | fix | |
   17: |---|---|---|---|
   18: | `walk off` | `0xaaaa0000aaaa` | `0xbbbb0000bbbb` | `vaddr+8` must return `data+8`, not `data+0` |
   19: | `walk vpn` | `0xcccc0000cccc` | `0xdddd0000dddd` | non-zero VPN, offset in the upper 4 KB |
   20: | five controls | ok | ok | two K0SEG reads, `wasn't found` absent, sentinel, not-unmapped |
   21: 
   22: **THE SECOND ROW EXISTS BECAUSE THE FIX REVIEW REFUSED THE ROUND WITHOUT IT.** With `VADDR = 0` and
   23: `N = 8`, **six lesser edits also score a clean sweep**: masks `0xfff`, `0x3fff`, `0x7fff`, any mask
   24: including bit 3, a hardcoded `| 8`, and **unmasked `+ vaddr`** - the last correct only because the VPN
   25: is zero on page 0. `walk vpn` kills the three that matter.
   26: 
   27: > **AND TWO OF THE SIX ARE DELIBERATELY NOT KILLED.** `0x3fff` and `0x7fff` are **equivalent inside an
   28: > 8 KB page** - bits 13 and 14 of an offset are always zero there - so no row can distinguish them and
   29: > none pretends to. *A row that claimed to would be vacuous by construction.* Recorded in the probe.
   30: 
   31: **A THREE-WAY PLANT MAKES THE ROW DIAGNOSTIC RATHER THAN MERELY RED:** `VAL_B2` at `data2+0x1008` is
   32: correct, `VAL_C2` at `data2+8` is what a `0xfff` mask or a hardcoded `| 8` reads, `VAL_A2` at
   33: `data2+0` is a page-base result. HEAD read **`VAL_A2`**, naming the defect exactly.
   34: 
   35: **THE FORM OF THE FIX, and every clause of it was argued rather than assumed:**
   36: 
   37:     (*return_paddr) = (ALPHA_PTE_TO_PFN(pte3) << pageSizeShift)
   38:         | (vaddr & ((1ULL << pageSizeShift) - 1));
   39: 
   40: * **`|`, not `+`** - numerically identical here (the shifted PFN has bits 12:0 clear), but `|` is what
   41:   every other walk in this tree writes (ARM, m88k, MIPS, PPC, SH), while `+` is this function's own
   42:   *fixed* bootstrap windows. Different sentences.
   43: * **the mask derived from `pageSizeShift`, never a literal.** `:88`'s `0x1fff` is correct for a fixed
   44:   8 KB PROM pocket; beside `<< pageSizeShift` it is how a mask and its shift drift - and **this
   45:   function already carries two names for 13** (`ALPHA_PAGESHIFT` and the local `pageSizeShift`).
   46: * **the parentheses are load-bearing:** `1 << pageSizeShift - 1` is `0x1000`, not `0x1fff`. *The
   47:   existing `N = 8` row already kills that one* (`8 & 0x1000 == 0`).
   48: 
   49: **A THIRD CONSUMER, found by enumerating the callers rather than assuming one.** `translate_v2p` is
   50: called from three places: `memory_rw.c` (needs the full address - the defect), `cpu_dyntrans.c`
   51: I-fetch (tolerates either; strips the offset itself), and **`debugger_cmds.c` watchpoints, which take
   52: the address directly - so a walked watchpoint has been landing at the page base too.** No caller adds
   53: its own offset, so the mirror defect is absent and the post-fix sweep is not hiding a double-add.
   54: 
   55: **THE `// TODO.` STAYS, and the round says why.** Restoring the offset leaves it true: `flags` is
   56: never read (`FLAG_WRITEFLAG`, `FLAG_INSTR`, `FLAG_NOEXCEPTIONS` all ignored), `ALPHA_PTE_KR/KW/UR/UW`
   57: and `FAULT_ON_*` are untested, granularity hints are ignored, and **`return 2` means writable - so
   58: `memory_rw` caches every walked Alpha page as writable.** *Deleting the comment would record the
   59: translator as finished. It is not.*
   60: 
   61: **Trees:** `memory_alpha.c` is not in `tools/est_divergence.txt`, so it is shared and landed
   62: byte-identical in all four. `build/`, `build-asan/` and `/tmp/gxsec-build` rebuilt; two-part clearance
   63: CLEAN/FRESH on all three, `est/` part 2 N/A.
   64: 
   65: # `#507` alphaswpctxptbr: `swpctx` assigned `apcb_ptbr` twice and `apcb_flags` never
   66: 
   67: **THE DEFECT.** `cpu_alpha_palcode.c`, `case 0x30` PAL_OSF1_swpctx, load-new-context block:
   68: 
   69:     :278    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr    = new_ptbr;
   70:     :282    cpu->cd.alpha.pcb.apcb_ptbr    = new_flags;      <-- apcb_ptbr, again
   71: 
   72: A field-name transposition. The value is loaded correctly - the SAVE stores `apcb_flags` to
   73: `ctx + 40` and the loader reads `new_flags` from `ctx + 40` - and lands on the wrong left-hand side.
   74: 
   75: **FOUND BY COUNTING, NOT BY READING.** Over the block, `apcb_ptbr` is assigned **twice**,
   76: `apcb_flags` **zero** times, every other field exactly once. Reading it, `:282` looks like an ordinary
   77: assignment in a column of ordinary assignments and the eye supplies the field name it expects; a
   78: one-line census makes the 2-and-0 unmissable. **The same census is the fix's verification:**
   79: 
   80:     before   apcb_ptbr 2   apcb_flags 0     (every other field 1)
   81:     after    apcb_ptbr 1   apcb_flags 1     (every field exactly 1)
   82: 
   83: *The block's own comment at `:275` reads "Update all variables in the pcb simultaneously" - directly
   84: above the list that fails to. A claim of completeness is the least-checked line in a block, because
   85: it reads as a description of the code beneath it.*
   86: 
   87: **THREE CONSEQUENCES, and the row originally recorded one.**
   88: 1. `apcb_ptbr` ends up holding the flags word - the clobber the witness first measured.
   89: 2. `apcb_flags` is never loaded, so the incoming context's flags are dropped.
   90: 3. **The SAVE then publishes that never-loaded word into the OUTGOING PCB**, wiping the flags the
   91:    guest planted. *The bug propagates outward into guest memory, not only inward into the cpu struct.*
   92: 
   93: **WITNESS, rung 3, unmodified `-E alpha -e alphabook1`:** `regress/alpha_harness_probe.py`.
   94: **HEAD 7/10** (`probe_head10_20260907.out`), **fix 10/10, same script** (`probe_fix_20260907.out`):
   95: 
   96: | row | HEAD | fix | |
   97: |---|---|---|---|
   98: | `swpctx ptbr` | `0xbbbb2222` | `0xaaaa1111` | `PCB_A+16` must hold A.ptbr |
   99: | `swpctx notflags` | `0xbbbb2222` | `0xaaaa1111` | the defect's exact signature |
  100: | `swpctx flags` | **`0x0`** | **`0xbbbb2222`** | `PCB_A+40` must hold A.flags |
  101: | six controls | ok | ok | encoder, live, exec, palgate, swpctx alive, swpctx mapped |
  102: 
  103: > ### *** THE THIRD ROW EXISTS BECAUSE THE FIX REVIEW REFUSED TO RATIFY THE ROUND WITHOUT IT. ***
  104: >
  105: > grok ratified the one-token product fix and then declined to call it the round: *"Both red rows read
  106: > the same `got`: `A+16`. A build that only stops clobbering PTBR - delete `:282`, or assign
  107: > `new_flags` to any other field - also scores 9/9. **Consequence 2 is unpinned.**"*
  108: >
  109: > That is a clause-(d) gap in the WITNESS rather than in the fix, and the repair it named cost one
  110: > readback on a page `swpctx mapped` had already proved was backed: `A+40` is `0` on HEAD (a save of a
  111: > never-loaded field) and `FLAGS_VAL` after. **Three red rows now pin three distinct consequences
  112: > instead of one cell read twice.**
  113: 
  114: **NO READER RELIES ON THE STALE FIELD.** Every `apcb_flags` site in `src/` was enumerated: `wrfen`
  115: `:221` writes the whole word without reading it; `:222-223` publishes what it just stored; the SAVE at
  116: `:243-244` publishes to the outgoing PCB (which today propagates the bug and after the fix carries the
  117: loaded value - the PAL contract); `ALPHA_PCB_FLAGS_FEN`, `_PME` and `ALPHA_IF_CODE_FEN` are header-only
  118: and unused; `memory_alpha.c:46` reads `apcb_ptbr`, not flags. *Sticky-FEN-across-`swpctx` is a PAL bug,
  119: not a behaviour a reader is entitled to.*
  120: 
  121: **TWO PREMISES OF MINE CORRECTED BY THE REVIEW**, both in the direction of less confidence:
  122: `apcb_flags` **is** written globally (by `wrfen`), so "never assigned at all" holds only inside the
  123: load block; and the in-cpu value is **last `wrfen` or 0 from the zeroed cpu struct**, not "whatever
  124: the previous context left" - `swpctx` has never loaded that field from any PCB.
  125: 
  126: **FILED IN PASSING, not fixed here:** `PAL_clrfen` (`0xae`) is named and user-mode-whitelisted but has
  127: **no `case`**, so it reaches the default arm and stops the CPU.
  128: 
  129: **Trees:** `cpu_alpha_palcode.c` is not in `tools/est_divergence.txt`, so it is shared and landed
  130: byte-identical in all four. `build/`, `build-asan/` and `/tmp/gxsec-build` rebuilt; two-part clearance
  131: CLEAN/FRESH on all three, `est/` part 2 N/A (source tree - inapplicable, not unrun).
  132: 
  133: # `#506` lcaconfwidth: the LCA kill diagnostic named a value the guard ACCEPTS
  134: 
  135: **THE DEFECT.** `dev_lca.c`'s `LCA_IOC_CONF` write arm stores the full 64-bit value, guards on all 64
  136: bits, and reports 32:
  137: 
  138:     d->ioc_conf = idata;                              /* the FULL value is stored */
  139:     if (idata & ~1) {                                 /* the guard sees 64 bits   */
  140:             fatal("TODO: Write to unimplemented bit of"
  141:                 " IOC_CONF: 0x%x\n", (int)idata);      /* the message sees 32     */
  142:             exit(1);
  143:     }
  144: 
  145: A guest `stq` of `0x100000000` sets bit 32. `idata & ~1` is true, so the process dies - and
  146: `(int)idata` is **0**, which is a value this same guard **accepts**. *The kill message named an
  147: acceptable value while killing on an unacceptable one*, in the one place a wrong record is hardest to
  148: recover from: the process is gone and the log is all that is left.
  149: 
  150: **WITNESS, rung 3, on an unmodified `-E alpha -e alphabook1`:** `regress/lca_confwidth_probe.py`.
  151: **HEAD 4/6** (`regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/probe_head_20260907.out`), **fix 6/6, same script**
  152: (`probe_fix_20260907.out`) - clause (i) satisfied by the pair, not by argument:
  153: 
  154: | row | HEAD | fix | |
  155: |---|---|---|---|
  156: | `live` | alive | alive | control: the session survives the legal write |
  157: | `ioc rt` | `0x1` | `0x1` | control: guest `stq 0x1` then guest `ldq`, SAME decode |
  158: | `kill fired` | yes | yes | control: the IOC_CONF arm announced - not HAE, not unmapped |
  159: | `kill width` | **`0x0`** | **`0x100000000`** | the message must name the value that ACTUALLY killed |
  160: | `kill notzero` | **`0x0`** | wider | `0x0` is a value this guard ACCEPTS |
  161: | `died` | died | died | control: `exit(1)` really ran |
  162: 
  163: **THE FIX IS THE FILE'S OWN SIBLINGS, COPIED.** `TB_ENA`, `W_BASE0`, `W_MASK0`, `W_BASE1` and
  164: `W_MASK1` - twelve to ninety lines away - already print `0x%" PRIx64` with `(uint64_t)idata`. This arm
  165: was the outlier. `PRIx64` already resolved in the file (5 uses), so no include changed, and **no gate,
  166: hygiene grep or probe in `regress/` matches this message's text**, so changing it turns nothing else
  167: red.
  168: 
  169: **TWO SITES, AND THE RECORD DISTINGUISHES THEM RATHER THAN LUMPING THEM.**
  170: * `:205` **IOC_CONF** - the sharp instance. The guard is on the **value**, so the narrowed message can
  171:   name a value the guard would have accepted. This is what the witness measures.
  172: * `:319` **the `default:` arm** - the same narrowing, but its guard is on the **offset**, so the
  173:   printed value is wrong *without* being one the guard would have accepted. Fixed for consistency,
  174:   not because it can mislead the same way.
  175: 
  176: > ### *** THE FILED RECORD PRESCRIBED THE WRONG FIX **AND** CLAIMED IT MATCHED THE SURROUNDING CODE.
  177: > ### Corrected in `OUTSTANDING_BUGS.md` before the edit. ***
  178: >
  179: > It said the repair was `` `0x%llx` with a `(long long)` cast, the file's own idiom elsewhere ``.
  180: > **`dev_lca.c` uses `PRIx64` five times and `%llx` never**, and the siblings **keep** the cast rather
  181: > than dropping it. A design brief of mine then compounded it by saying to *drop* the cast - a third
  182: > wrong form. **A record that names a fix and asserts it matches the neighbours is the most costly
  183: > kind to get wrong: it discourages the one check - reading a sibling - that catches it.**
  184: 
  185: **FOUR DESIGN CORRECTIONS FROM THE GROK SEAT, each of which would have produced an unreadable or
  186: falsely-green run** (`regress/artifacts/lcaconfwidth/grok_witness2_20260907.txt`):
  187: * `load_const` cannot build either operand - LDA/LDAH make 32-bit sign-extended constants, the K0SEG
  188:   address needs bits 63..42 and the value needs bit 32. Both are planted with `put d` and loaded with
  189:   `ldq` while `ptbr` is still 0.
  190: * **Debugger `dump` cannot be the read-back.** It calls `memory_rw` with `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, and LCA
  191:   registers `DM_DEFAULT` rather than `DM_READS_HAVE_NO_SIDE_EFFECTS`, so the handler is *skipped* and
  192:   the caller gets the pre-zeroed buffer - **the unmapped signature produced by a hit.**
  193: * **`put d` of the killing value is not rung 3.** `store_64bit_word` goes through `memory_rw` with
  194:   `CACHE_DATA` and no `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, so it enters the handler and `exit(1)`s - a real kill caused by
  195:   no guest instruction.
  196: * **A miss is not only "unmapped  nothing".** Offset 0 of the window is HAE, which `break`s with no
  197:   kill, so a store there is silent *inside* the LCA window. `ioc rt` is what separates the two.
  198: 
  199: **AND THE PROBE'S FIRST DRAFT SCORED 6/6 ON THE COMMITTED BUILD** because it asserted the DEFECT as
