15645: ## 2026-09-07 - `sgimtedepth4dead` raised LOW  HIGH: the dead branch is the MECHANISM, not a curiosity
15646: 
15647: **I FILED THIS SAYING "a dead branch cannot itself corrupt anything".  THAT IS WRONG.**  The finding was in the
15648: kimi seat's pass-1 answer - a file I had already opened and under-read - and is verified here:
15649: 
15650:     src_mode = (src_tlb << 10) + (((mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK) >> MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT) << 8);
15651:     dst_mode = (dst_tlb << 10) + (((mode & MTE_MODE_DEPTH_MASK) >> MTE_DEPTH_SHIFT) << 8);
15652:     if (depth == 4) {                     <- DEAD: depth = 8 << field, so 8/16/32/64, never 4
15653:             src_mode |= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA;
15654:             dst_mode |= DE_MODE_TYPE_RGBA;
15655:     }
15656: 
15657: The constructed modes carry **no colour bits**, so the colour type is `0x00` = **CI**, and the only thing that
15658: would make them RGBA is the dead branch.  **So the MTE path always runs CI** - and CI's PUT arm fills only
15659: `buf[0]` while the transfer is `bufdepth` bytes:
15660: 
15661:     MTE_DEPTH_8  = 0 -> bufdepth 1   matched, no leak
15662:     MTE_DEPTH_16 = 1 -> bufdepth 2   fills buf[0], writes 2 -> 1 uninitialised byte per pixel
15663:     MTE_DEPTH_32 = 2 -> bufdepth 4   fills buf[0], writes 4 -> 3 uninitialised bytes per pixel
15664: 
15665: **All three are documented constants.**  kimi: *"every MTE_TLB_A/B/C fill with MTE_DEPTH_16/32 leaks
15666: buf[1..3] per pixel (documented MTE_DEPTH values!).  This is the strongest reachability evidence: PROM/NetBSD
15667: `crmfb_fill_rect` uses this path."*  That gives `sgirebufuninit` an **all-documented** reachability vehicle on
15668: a path a real driver uses - where every argument I had recorded relied on the DE path and conceded that PATH
15669: A's maximal form needs the undocumented depth field 3.
15670: 
15671: *** AND IT CHANGES THE FIX ORDER IN A WAY THAT WOULD HAVE BITTEN. ***  Repairing `depth == 4` **alone** would
15672: switch the MTE path to RGBA, which fills `buf[0..3]` - **accidentally masking the leak at depth 32** while
15673: leaving depth 16 (2 bytes, RGBA fills 4) and every DE-path case untouched.  A round fixing only this would
15674: *look* like it had fixed `sgirebufuninit` and would not have.  That is the shape the vacuity floor exists to
15675: catch, surfacing before either round starts.
15676: 
15677: **It also settles a correction I had recorded without noticing what it implied.**  The codex seat told me *"not
15678: all seven call sites receive those register words - the two MTE sites construct their own modes."*  I filed
15679: that as a narrowing of my reachability claim.  **Constructing them is exactly what makes them leak** - the two
15680: corrections are one fact seen from opposite sides.
15681: 
15682: **Both file-reading seats independently reached the same detector requirement:** the oracle must assert
15683: **values, not determinism**.  kimi's proof is the sharper: post-fix PATH B yields a deterministic value *and*
15684: the `bufdepth`-sized mutant yields the previous pixel's bytes, which are **also** deterministic under stack
